<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gangeyadeva</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1015–1041</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gangeyadeva was a king of the Kalachuris of Tripuri in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the early 11th century. He took the title Vikramaditya and expanded the kingdom northwards, extending his rule as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>. His gold coins, showing a seated goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, were widely imitated by later dynasties of northern India. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmikarna">Lakshmikarna</a> brought the kingdom to its height.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gangeyadeva.jpg" alt="Gangeyadeva" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalachuris_of_Tripuri._Gangeya._Circa_1015-1040._Lakshmi_seated_facing_-_%E2%80%9C%C5%9Arimad_Gangeya_Devah%E2%80%9D_in_three_lines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gangeyadeva succeeded his father Kokalla II on the throne of Tripuri around 1015, ruling a kingdom centred on the Chedi or Dahala region in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>. In his Mukundpur inscription of 1019 he used only the modest titles Maharha-Maha-Mahattaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a>, which suggests he was then a feudatory, possibly of the Paramara king Bhoja. Allied with Bhoja and Rajendra Chola, he fought the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayasimha-ii">Jayasimha II</a> of Kalyani; both sides claimed success, and the allies seem to have been forced back after early gains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Paramara records say Bhoja defeated the ruler of Chedi, and historians disagree whether this came before or after the Chalukya war. In the 1030s Gangeyadeva raided his neighbours and made himself sovereign: his Piawan rock inscription of 1037-38 gives him the imperial titles Paramabhattaraka Maharajadhiraja Parameshvara. The Persian writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-biruni">Al-Biruni</a> names him as ruler of the Dahala country with his capital at Tiauri, that is, Tripuri.</p><p>In the east he invaded Utkala, probably helped by the dynasty's Ratnapura branch, and fought Yayati II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakshina-kosala">Dakshina Kosala</a> without a decisive result. As the Chandelas weakened under Ghaznavid invasions he pushed north, and even Chandela records call him jita-vishva, world-conqueror. The chronicler Baihqui shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> under his rule in 1033-1034, when Ahmad Niyaltigin, the Ghaznavid governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, plundered the city until noon and then withdrew, probably as the Kalachuri army approached.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kalachuri records say he attained salvation under the sacred banyan, the Akshayavata, at Prayaga, and that his hundred wives immolated themselves on his pyre. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmikarna">Lakshmikarna</a>, or Karna, issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> inscription in 1042 for his father's first death anniversary, which places his death on 22 January 1041. He installed a shivalinga at Piawan, which suggests he was a Shaivite, and his coins bore his name on one side and the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a> on the other.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gangeyadeva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
